WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.790
Today I have Steve from Steve's Point of
View, and that's not my point of view,

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:11.010
it's Steve who has a channel which he
beams to Japan from the United States and

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:15.240
talks about all kinds of things, including
cars, but he is fluent in Japanese.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.600
And I would like to find out from
Steve, and you can begin then

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.530
by introducing yourself, like,
what is it that you do right now?

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.480
How did you learn Japanese?

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.200
And how did you manage to turn
your knowledge of Japanese into an

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.270
activity that is a viable business.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:34.900
Hajimemashite Steve-san.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.110
Hajimemashite, yoroshiku onegaishimasu.

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:38.175
Uh, thanks, uh, for
having me on your channel.

00:00:38.175 --> 00:00:38.625
It's an honor.

00:00:38.625 --> 00:00:39.765
I appreciate you having me.

00:00:39.765 --> 00:00:40.995
My name is Steve.

00:00:41.055 --> 00:00:45.135
Um, I have a channel called Steve's
Point of View or Steve's POV for short.

00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:47.655
I am fluent in Japanese.

00:00:47.685 --> 00:00:50.385
Um, uh, but I didn't start
off my life that way.

00:00:50.435 --> 00:00:53.505
I started studying Japanese, I
really wasn't interested in Japan

00:00:53.505 --> 00:00:57.135
until I got to college in 1988
at the university of Wisconsin.

00:00:57.165 --> 00:00:59.940
I was a business major
there studying business.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.010
And at the time Japan's
economy was booming.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.980
Uh, there were books being
written about Japan becoming the

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.420
number one economy in the world.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.010
It was fascinating for sure.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:11.500
Uh, and as a business major, I, I,
my goal was to make money and to

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:12.780
try to be successful in business.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.370
And I thought by combining, uh, Japanese
language with my business degree, it

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:22.730
would be a way for me to achieve, uh,
success, uh, working together with Japan.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.070
And, uh, that was my stepping
stone and I got to Japan in

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.259
the summer, uh, back in 1991.

00:01:28.259 --> 00:01:32.670
I spent three months on a chicken farm
in rural Japan, immersing myself into

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:37.110
the culture, avoided speaking English
for three months and came back to America

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:42.645
with a, uh, a strong, uh, confidence,
I guess that I can learn this language.

00:01:42.675 --> 00:01:44.925
I can master the language,
the culture as well.

00:01:44.925 --> 00:01:48.285
I can get the, learn it very well
and, uh, be successful that way.

00:01:48.285 --> 00:01:51.815
And I won a contest in 1993 called
the Japanese Speech Language Contest.

00:01:51.815 --> 00:01:55.725
It's a national contest held in
Chicago and that, that got me, uh,

00:01:55.755 --> 00:01:59.865
back to Japan and got me my first job
opportunity there in Japan in 1995.

00:01:59.905 --> 00:02:02.775
Interrupt you for just a second, because
you said something that's so important.

00:02:02.955 --> 00:02:06.065
You came back confident that
you could learn the language.

00:02:06.485 --> 00:02:11.165
I think that's an absolute key, that, that
sense that I can do this thing anyway.

00:02:11.165 --> 00:02:11.825
Sorry to interrupt.

00:02:11.825 --> 00:02:13.864
But I just thought, I thought
that was such an important thing.

00:02:13.865 --> 00:02:15.215
It's a fantastic point, Steve.

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:18.405
I wasn't confident until I went
to Japan, I was not confident.

00:02:18.425 --> 00:02:21.035
I was one of the kids in the class
who didn't have any experience

00:02:21.035 --> 00:02:24.785
with, with cultural exchanges or,
or homestays or anything like that.

00:02:24.785 --> 00:02:26.225
A lot of kids in the class did.

00:02:26.435 --> 00:02:26.855
I didn't.

00:02:26.855 --> 00:02:31.135
I felt that I, I wasn't very good until I
got to Japan and spent that three months.

00:02:31.335 --> 00:02:32.640
And really that was the confidence.

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:35.079
I came back with that confidence
that I could learn this language.

00:02:35.079 --> 00:02:35.750
I could speak it.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:36.620
I could pronounce it.

00:02:36.859 --> 00:02:38.750
I can, I could, I could do well with it.

00:02:39.109 --> 00:02:42.200
And then, you know, there's another
point of language  you maybe understand

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.450
that they say at some point, when
you're learning a language, you

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:45.799
start to dream in the language.

00:02:45.799 --> 00:02:48.620
You start to, you start to speak
the language in your dreams.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:51.769
And that, after that point is when
I started actually having dreams

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:55.700
in Japanese and somebody told me at
that point, that's when you know you,

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:59.000
I just mastered or to some degree,
you know, you've got the language.

00:02:59.805 --> 00:03:03.165
And, uh, that day came and, uh, and
from there I got back to Japan, started

00:03:03.225 --> 00:03:07.454
working, uh, worked, uh, for a Japanese
company for about a year and a half

00:03:07.454 --> 00:03:09.765
or so before I ventured off on my own.

00:03:10.095 --> 00:03:14.295
And I started, uh, doing, uh, translating
and importing and exporting and all

00:03:14.295 --> 00:03:19.755
kinds of stuff in Japan as a foreigner,
uh, back in the early nineties, uh, long

00:03:19.755 --> 00:03:23.295
way through there, back to the United
States and California to now, to Texas.

00:03:23.475 --> 00:03:25.725
But I started YouTube
about seven years ago.

00:03:25.935 --> 00:03:30.465
Uh, the passion of doing so was to
show people, uh, there is a Japanese

00:03:30.465 --> 00:03:35.415
speaking, um, non-Japanese person here
in north America who, uh, understands

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:38.295
business and would love to work
together and, and venture with people.

00:03:38.295 --> 00:03:40.859
And that was the purpose
of starting steve's POV.

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.890
And, uh, thankfully here,
about seven years later, almost

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.600
400,000 subscribers strong.

00:03:45.899 --> 00:03:49.320
Uh, the channel is, um, something
that's fun to do along with my real

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.060
estate business as well here in Texas.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:56.700
So you have a real estate business in
Texas, and then you have this, this

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:01.230
YouTube channel, and I know you're very
interested in cars, but, uh, do you

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:06.209
just talk about cars, uh, or does your
point of view include, you know, Steve,

00:04:06.239 --> 00:04:10.619
uh, you know, ranting on, like I do
on a variety of different subjects in

00:04:10.619 --> 00:04:14.929
Japanese, uh, that your Japanese audience
finds interesting, or is it mostly cars?

00:04:14.929 --> 00:04:18.510
Well, Um, lately it's been mostly
cars, but the channel started with

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:22.469
something from my POV, the point of
view that I had in living and working

00:04:22.469 --> 00:04:26.159
in Japan, living and experiencing
Japanese culture and being an American.

00:04:26.159 --> 00:04:31.080
And then coming back to the American,
America and with a fresh Japanese work

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:35.010
mindset, that way of doing things and
coming back to America and experiencing

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:38.850
a bit of culture shock, but even
as an American back in from Japan.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:42.390
And I started making videos
where I played a Japanese salary

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:46.750
man and a Japanese salesman in
the same kind of short parad...

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:48.030
parody type setting.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:52.030
And it shows kind of those big
differences between Japan and America.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:55.490
Sorry, did you say a Japanese
salary man and a Japanese salesman?

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:55.930
American salesman.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:56.440
American Salesman.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:58.030
American salesman versus
Japanese salesman.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:04:58.460
Okay, sorry.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:04:58.840
No problem.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:04:59.050
Sorry.

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:00.040
I might've missed that.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:03.070
Um, so, and you know, they're
very different Japanese, you

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:06.130
know, the necktie's on you're
very, you know, you're suited up.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:07.120
You're, you're very stiff.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:07.930
You're very rigid.

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:10.719
You're very manual in many
ways, there's way to do things.

00:05:10.719 --> 00:05:12.190
And there's only one way
to do things and yeah.

00:05:13.395 --> 00:05:15.525
And then Americans were a little
more laid back, but you know,

00:05:15.525 --> 00:05:17.085
my feet are up on the desk guys.

00:05:17.085 --> 00:05:21.015
T-shirts on Fridays and we're out of the
office at 2.30, we got a baseball game

00:05:21.015 --> 00:05:23.604
for the kids, you know, it's, uh, it,
these are things in Japan you didn't have.

00:05:23.625 --> 00:05:25.844
I found those differences
very interesting.

00:05:25.844 --> 00:05:28.305
And I put some videos out
about them that did very well.

00:05:29.474 --> 00:05:33.914
You know, one of the big differences
in my experience is in Japan, if you

00:05:33.914 --> 00:05:36.974
are dealing with someone in business,
then you say, can you do this?

00:05:37.335 --> 00:05:41.535
And they said, "Hmm, do
desho", very difficult.

00:05:41.565 --> 00:05:42.675
That means he's going to do it.

00:05:43.185 --> 00:05:45.285
Uh, in the north America.

00:05:45.385 --> 00:05:46.635
If you say, can you do this?

00:05:46.665 --> 00:05:47.625
Oh yeah, no problem.

00:05:48.195 --> 00:05:50.925
Uh, maybe, and maybe not.

00:05:51.045 --> 00:05:52.035
Maybe, maybe not.

00:05:52.035 --> 00:05:53.445
And everything's the best here.

00:05:53.445 --> 00:05:54.865
You know, we talk about us a lot.

00:05:54.865 --> 00:05:55.515
Oh the best.

00:05:55.515 --> 00:05:56.865
And it's mine.

00:05:57.015 --> 00:05:57.915
That's very personalized.

00:05:57.915 --> 00:05:59.955
You could be working for a company
here, but you talking about my

00:05:59.955 --> 00:06:04.200
warehouse, my products, my guy in
the warehouse, my guy in the back,

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.490
you know, it's very personalized.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:05.970
You're in Japan.

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:06.960
It's about the group.

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:07.890
It's about the company.

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:10.720
It's about the bigger, uh, entity.

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:16.020
Plus in Japan, they don't necessarily,
because you say my product is the best,

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:20.430
that, they don't necessarily believe that,
you know, just because you say yours is

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:25.289
the best, you may not be convincing anyone
and maybe you want to try to persuade

00:06:25.289 --> 00:06:28.770
them that you're going to try hard to,
you know, look after your customer,

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:30.440
rather than just saying mine is the best.

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:33.810
There's a bit more of a humble approach,
I think, to, uh, to what you have there.

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:34.219
Yep, definitely.

00:06:34.219 --> 00:06:38.490
It's not that hard, maybe sales push,
or maybe as hard of a, you know,

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:41.070
there's the best you've got to try the
better, it's more of a humble approach.

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:43.260
And you know, that's all
built into the culture too.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:46.710
So when you learn Japanese language,
right, Steve, it's not just learning

00:06:46.999 --> 00:06:51.705
"nani nani desu", "nani nani masu" you
know, you know, the basic Japanese words,

00:06:51.705 --> 00:06:53.715
but it's, it's learning that culture.

00:06:53.715 --> 00:06:57.795
And then the hierarchal Japanese society
and how you humble yourself or elevate

00:06:57.825 --> 00:07:02.385
others, and how you talk to somebody older
than yourself, or a teacher or a Shōchō,

00:07:02.745 --> 00:07:06.585
a president in a, in a company versus the
guy who works below you in the company.

00:07:06.585 --> 00:07:07.665
It's very different.

00:07:07.665 --> 00:07:09.795
It's a different mindset
that you need to understand.

00:07:11.205 --> 00:07:14.385
But, but, uh, one thing I would say
about that is that first of all, because

00:07:14.414 --> 00:07:17.985
Japan has had a different history and
is from a very different culture, it

00:07:17.985 --> 00:07:22.005
doesn't mean that a North American
can't learn that just as a Japanese

00:07:22.005 --> 00:07:24.224
person can learn the North American way.

00:07:24.825 --> 00:07:28.125
And so I definitely feel that,
I mean, I can go over there and

00:07:28.125 --> 00:07:29.775
feel I'm integrated in with them.

00:07:29.775 --> 00:07:32.534
I mean, they look at me, I look a
little different, but culturally, in

00:07:32.534 --> 00:07:35.325
terms of what we're saying to each
other, we're all operating kind of

00:07:35.325 --> 00:07:36.905
in the same cultural environment.

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:38.550
Uh, that was one point.

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:42.180
And I think another important point
is you can't read a book which

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:44.640
tells you how you should behave.

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:48.810
And when you use the polite form
and then expect to apply that you

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:52.980
actually have to live it until
you start to feel it naturally.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:54.270
Exactly true.

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:57.120
And that's part of going there and,
you know, there's a lot of foreigners

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:59.969
that were in Japan, uh, now, you know,
and over the years, more and more

00:07:59.969 --> 00:08:02.670
have increased and you see a lot of
foreigners stay together with other

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:06.210
foreigners that there's, you know, there
are people who are with Japanese, of

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:09.659
course, but if you really want to learn,
you've got to go out with Japanese in

00:08:09.659 --> 00:08:13.170
different social situations, whether
it's eating, whether it's drinking,

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:16.770
whether it's studying together or
whatever, it may be working together and

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:23.265
to understand those non-language, the
non-language language, and the gestures.

00:08:23.505 --> 00:08:26.115
And, but some of the differences,
differences that are there that

00:08:26.115 --> 00:08:28.605
unless you experience it, like
you said, you can't just learn it

00:08:28.605 --> 00:08:30.095
in, in a, in a book necessarily.

00:08:30.775 --> 00:08:31.725
No, no.

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:35.220
And I think it's important to then
want to be part of that group.

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:37.110
And the same applies in reverse.

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:40.860
If a Japanese person comes to North
America, you have to want, or anybody

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:43.799
learning any language, you have
to want to be part of that group.

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:47.850
And, and it's it's, if you're very
self-conscious of yourself, here I

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:51.330
am, I'm an American or a Canadian
I'm in amongst all these Japanese.

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:54.510
And, and somehow the fact that
I'm this American speaking

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:56.090
Japanese is somehow important.

00:08:56.595 --> 00:08:57.615
All of that is negative.

00:08:57.615 --> 00:09:01.275
You're just there, one of them, part
of the group, uh, that's been my

00:09:01.275 --> 00:09:05.415
experience that leads to acquiring the
language and feeling part of a group.

00:09:05.535 --> 00:09:08.385
If you go there and you act like, you
know, Hey, well, I'm an American, I

00:09:08.385 --> 00:09:09.705
do things differently and all that.

00:09:09.705 --> 00:09:11.385
So you're, you're gonna,
you're going to fail.

00:09:11.385 --> 00:09:15.405
I, I, in my opinion, in, in,
um, in really understanding and

00:09:15.405 --> 00:09:17.835
appreciating and learning Japanese
culture and the beauty of it.

00:09:17.835 --> 00:09:20.715
I think you need to go overe
there as if somebody comes here,

00:09:20.715 --> 00:09:23.145
it's not, you know, you have your
culture, you have your identity.

00:09:23.145 --> 00:09:23.655
That's great.

00:09:23.655 --> 00:09:25.605
But you know, you're in their country.

00:09:25.605 --> 00:09:27.015
You're a guest in that country.

00:09:27.405 --> 00:09:30.015
There are certain rules and certain
formal things that are done.

00:09:30.015 --> 00:09:32.925
And believe me, if you don't
follow those, you'll see maybe some

00:09:32.925 --> 00:09:35.715
people say, well, he's American,
you know, or he's a foreigner.

00:09:35.715 --> 00:09:37.335
So, you know, we'll give him a pass on it.

00:09:37.335 --> 00:09:40.125
But if you're there and really trying to
assimilate and you're doing stupid stuff,

00:09:40.155 --> 00:09:41.205
you're going to get called out on it.

00:09:42.935 --> 00:09:47.025
But I think though it's important
not to over sort of dramatize this.

00:09:47.025 --> 00:09:50.505
Like sometimes you'll get Americans going
over there, they're bowing 90 degrees.

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:54.060
You know, you have to feel it.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:59.430
You have to have, you know, sensitive
tenticles, antenna that, that

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:01.290
you start to feel it naturally.

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:02.820
And so it's comfortable doing it.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:04.800
You don't want to be doing
something that's uncomfortable.

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:07.500
I mean, of course you'd take your
shoes off before you walk in a house.

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:10.650
But other than that, I don't think
people need to be so uptight.

00:10:11.395 --> 00:10:15.025
If they just naturally connect with
people, person to person, they'll

00:10:15.025 --> 00:10:18.745
gradually start to see how other people
behave and start behaving the same way.

00:10:18.865 --> 00:10:22.705
Just as we acquire language through
imitation, we acquire that, that

00:10:22.735 --> 00:10:27.235
cultural communication skill
through just letting it come.

00:10:27.295 --> 00:10:27.535
You know?

00:10:27.565 --> 00:10:28.255
I agree with you.

00:10:28.285 --> 00:10:28.525
Yeah.

00:10:28.825 --> 00:10:29.335
Tell me...

00:10:29.335 --> 00:10:33.535
Please go ahead with your question
but I agree with your point.

00:10:33.535 --> 00:10:34.675
No I was going to say, what is it now?

00:10:34.675 --> 00:10:39.925
What is the appeal to your viewer base,
400,000 subscribers, that's a lot.

00:10:40.455 --> 00:10:44.535
Uh, what is it you think that they
find appealing in your videos?

00:10:45.105 --> 00:10:47.265
I think they, they find
appealing a couple of things.

00:10:47.265 --> 00:10:50.195
One, the fact that I'm located
in the United States, I'm, I'm

00:10:50.235 --> 00:10:54.315
not Japanese, but I'm doing my,
provide my shows in Japanese.

00:10:54.315 --> 00:10:58.185
That I've raised a son here in the
United States who was born in California,

00:10:58.185 --> 00:11:01.715
now in Texas is 21 years old
who speaks Japanese fluently.

00:11:02.105 --> 00:11:06.510
He's comfortable doing it and he's not,
um, um, ashamed or embarrassed in any way.

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:09.120
I think there's something unique
and completely different about that.

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:13.110
Um, I also try to point out in my videos,
even if they're about cars, the cultural

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:17.610
differences, whether it be how a box is
packed, that came from Japan and how it's

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:21.329
wrapped and presented versus something
that showed up from just down the street

00:11:21.329 --> 00:11:24.780
that looks like it just went through,
you know, all around the world and under

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:26.130
the ocean and everywhere else and back.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:30.180
You know, these, these things, I
try, I think people maybe find that

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:34.590
point of view interesting, um, a
father, son relationship as well.

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:35.790
Certainly part of it as well.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:37.320
Okay.

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:37.590
Yeah.

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:39.570
No, the Japanese attention to detail.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:42.380
If they're going to do something,
they're going to do it 110 percent.

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:45.360
Whereas they're in north
America, 65% is good.

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:45.480
Maybe

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:47.060
Sometimes.

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:48.270
We shouldn't be...yeah.

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:50.370
Okay.

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:54.060
Well, uh, we're going to have a chat
on your channel in Japanese, and I'm

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:57.690
certainly going to leave a link to
your channel in my description box.

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:02.250
